00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.340
minute presentations today and then we
can go from there.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.290
so we have some changes that are coming
up with the sequence databases and this

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:44.180
is a first webinar. we have a few other
announcements that have come out about

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.079
this but we're starting to try to do
some outreach about these changes so

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:51.110
that you have time to adjust to what's
going to be happening.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:54.920
so the first thing i'll try and talk
about is what's changing

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:59.120
what's going on and the short answer to
that question is that we're phasing out a

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.380
kind of identifier called a GI number,
and i'll talk more about what those are

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:04.579
a couple minutes.

00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:08.930
that's the next topic what they are a
little bit of a review of what kind of

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.400
identifiers that we use for the sequence
databases and why those changes are

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.790
happening and when they will happen. and
then the fourth topic

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.900
perhaps more interesting one, what's
going to break. if you have a piece of

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.580
software or service

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.090
what will break as a result of these
changes, won't break and we'll talk

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:29.930
briefly about that as well as some
additional service changes that we

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:34.310
anticipate that will be coming up in the
next weeks and months that are related

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.570
to these changes to

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:42.110
GI numbers. so what is changing? so we are
beginning to phase out the usage of the

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:45.710
GI number which has been a sequence
identifier used in the sequence databases

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.720
for many many years.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:53.210
the one thing to remember about this is
that existing GI numbers, records that

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:54.890
already have a GI number,

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.310
those GI numbers will remain
indefinitely alright so we're not

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.680
deleting GI numbers from data, they will
be able to be used for retreaving those

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.110
sequences that have them already but the
real issue is that ncbi will not be

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:14.120
assigning GI numbers to all new
sequences that are being submitted. and

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.860
over time it will

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.000
eventually happen that we will not
assign GI numbers at all anymore. that's

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.110
going to be a gradual process and only
will affect new sequences.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:30.830
what's happening soon in September is
that we will no longer show GI numbers

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:36.890
on flat file record views and fasta
record views by default. so those GI

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:42.140
numbers simply will not be present in
those data formats. the GI numbers will

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:46.070
still be present in the data record but
will not be visible in the flat file and

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.160
fasta formats. in march of 2017

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:54.170
you will not be able to see flat file or
fasta formats with GI numbers at

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:54.950
all.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.959
so beginning in September 2016 they will
not be by default but you will still be

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:02.720
able to get these records with GI
numbers in them.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.340
I will have more about that as we go
along but coming in March 2017 you won't

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.380
be able to see the GI numbers at all.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:13.310
so you have about seven months of leeway
to prepare yourself for the fact that

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:18.410
you will no longer be able to get the GI
numbers out of flat files or fasta. so

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:22.730
I want to take on a brief moment to show
you the original announcement that came

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.549
out about this in March of this year.

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:29.480
there's a link to it here. i'm going to just switch over to a web browser

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.390
to the article itself.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:37.519
this article is on the NCBI news page
it's the feature article on the NCBI news

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:38.150
site.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:42.950
It's just www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/news.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.060
you can see it there. i'm not going to
read through the whole article but i do

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.799
want to show you in the article there's
a couple of examples that kind of make

00:03:51.799 --> 00:03:52.730
the point.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:57.590
so this is part of a bit of a typical
nucleotide record in the nuccore

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:58.580
database.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.320
what you'll see is the two identifiers
that you see now, the GI number and the

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:08.000
accession number. and you also see these
for the protein product in the feature

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.200
table down here, the accession number for
the protein and the GI number for the

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:13.160
protein.

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.870
what will be happening is that those GI
numbers simply will be gone, right, they

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:22.370
will not be shown in the records. you'll
simply see the accession 
71
00:04:22,370 --&gt; 00:04:26,900
for the protein id,
there will not be a GI number present. a

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:27.600
similar

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.920
thing will happen for the protein
records. again right now in a protein

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.240
record you'll see the accession number
and the GI in the same line. in the

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.140
future

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:43.200
the GI number will not be there.
similarly in fasta formatted records

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.850
currently, the def line of the fasta
or the title line of the fasta has a

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.440
seq ID identifier at the beginning which
starts with the gi and then a pipe and

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.190
then the GI number and then a pipe and
then a little code for the source

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:00.480
database, for example genbank in this
case, a pipe and then the accession. what will

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.750
happen in the future is that you'll
simply have the accession there and

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.620
nothing else.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:11.580
so the g is gone, the pipes are gone. it's
much simpler, much more easy to parse but

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.090
the gi's are'nt present. so that's what
you're getting ready for, right, is

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.900
flat files and fasta records that don't
have the gi numbers present.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:25.290
I'm going to go back to the slides here
and talk a little bit about what gi's are.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:32.100
so just to review what gi's are and why
we've used them. so gi's are integers.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.230
they point to a particular version of a
sequence record and you'll find them in

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:44.280
a variety of databases. so principally
in Entrez nuccore, nucEST, nucGSS, protein

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:48.840
and popset. all have records that are
identified with GI numbers.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.190
this of course includes the genbank
database in its entirety and the RefSeq

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:58.500
database in its entirety. all of those
records also assign GI numbers and any

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.630
of those records on the web have a
report called a revision history which

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:08.310
I'm showing here. and revision history's
basically let you look at the history of

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:14.970
the versions of a particular record. this
record has an accession DQ656054,

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:19.500
but as you see here its had two GI
numbers in its history. because if a

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:24.780
sequence record changes the sequence
data, the sequence data itself is updated,

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.720
they're updated and the GI number will
shift.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:31.320
so what's happened with this record it
came in 2006,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.820
there was one update but the GI number
didn't change, that's because the update

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.320
wasn't to the sequence.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.370
it could have been to the author lists,
it could have been to an added

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:43.460
publication, it could have been an
editorial change or to new annotation,

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:48.500
but the sequence itself didn't change.
But the sequence did change in august of

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:54.080
two thousand seven. when that changes the
GI is updated to a new number and the

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.620
version of the accession is also
incremented.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:04.699
so now we have DQ656054.2, the second
version, as opposed to DQ656054.1.

00:07:04.699 --> 00:07:09.590
so you can view this history for any
record in any of these databases

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:13.880
just go to the upper left and you can
pull down the menu and get to revision

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:16.010
history report.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:21.710
so the point about accessions vs gi's is
that accessions point to a particular

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:27.320
version of a record, if you include the
version suffix, and the GI points to that

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:32.780
same record. So a given record, a
given version of a record, has always had

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:39.800
two identifiers, the GI number and the
accession.version. so the DQ656054.1

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.949
points to that particular GI number.

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:47.510
the updated record DQ656054.2 points to a different GI.

00:07:48.169 --> 00:07:51.500
let's say that in the future that record
is updated again.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.310
what would happen, could we predict what
would happen? From the accession number,

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:59.539
yes, we can predict because it will be
the same accession but it will be .3

00:07:59.539 --> 00:08:03.500
now, so the version increments. again we
have no way of knowing what the GI

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.370
number will be. effectively it will
simply be in a pseudo random number from

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:12.289
a user's point of view as to what that
actual identifier would be. the final

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:16.039
point to make about the accessions
versus the GI'S is a bare accession

00:08:16.039 --> 00:08:20.000
without the version, what does that
refer to, that's a really good question because

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.440
that accession number currently actually
points to two different versions in

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:27.560
Entrez. if you do a search with the
bare accession you'll get the most

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.410
recent version at the time of your
search,

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:35.750
but if you do the search today and six
months later the record is updated to .3

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:39.800
and you do the search again you'll now
get a different sequence back.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:44.570
alright so again accession.versions
are better to use than accessions because

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:50.390
they point to specific versions, just as
GI's, so that's basically where we are

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:53.170
in terms of accession numbers versus GI
numbers.

00:08:53.170 --> 00:09:00.190
why are we changing? a number of reasons.
one of the reasons is we have two

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.690
systems, GI numbers and accessions, and
that's not as good as having one

00:09:04.690 --> 00:09:06.160
consistent system.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.610
we're trying to move to one system of
identifiers which would be the 

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:13.480
accession.version. Accession.versions are more predictable. in other words when they get

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:16.510
updated you know what that's going to be,
you're going to increment the version

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:20.589
suffix. the accession will stay the same,
and they're more human readable.

00:09:20.589 --> 00:09:23.260
They have strings in them, the letters at the beginning, that can give you some

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:27.310
information about the type of sequence.
and we have a lot more variety of things

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:32.320
that we can use as accessions. so that's one
other reason to prefer them. they're

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.220
better able to handle the large variety
of sequence data that we have coming in

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.389
and the amount of that data coming in.
and another reason why we prefer

00:09:40.389 --> 00:09:44.170
accession.version at this point in time is, there are

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:48.519
already a large number of sequence
records that do not have gi numbers

00:09:48.519 --> 00:09:50.320
assigned to them and never will.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:55.029
for example wgs contigs, none of those
have gi numbers.

00:09:55.029 --> 00:10:00.640
TSA contigs, none of those have gi
numbers, but all of them have accession

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:05.709
.version. so a wgs accession is a long
affair, it has four letters at the

00:10:05.709 --> 00:10:09.910
beginning and then a series of numbers
and then a version suffix. tsa

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.470
accessions are very similar.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:16.540
so all of our sequence records
throughout ncbi can be represented

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:21.430
currently with an accession number .
version, but only a subset by GI.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:25.329
so that's another reason why were moving
towards accession.versions as the

00:10:25.329 --> 00:10:28.420
central and unifying identifier.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.750
so what is the time frame of when all
these things are happening?

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:34.930
I've mentioned this a little bit at the
beginning but just to crystallize it.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:39.399
we had our initial announcement in march.
we had a blog post that came out on our

00:10:39.399 --> 00:10:45.100
ncbi insights blog just a few days ago,
and we're doing a webinar now. we'll

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.910
continue to do more of these webinars
and more blog posts over the coming

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:53.019
weeks and months as developments occur
with these changes.

00:10:53.589 --> 00:10:58.300
we are currently continuing to work on
many of the ramifications of these and

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:02.440
you'll hear about these over time so this
is just the beginning of a series of

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:05.529
things that will be letting the
community know about what will be

00:11:05.529 --> 00:11:06.060
happening with these.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.650
In September again gi's 
will be disappearing from default

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.380
sequence record views, so fasta and flat
files

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:17.250
default views. You will still be able to,
we understand right now, to have a

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:21.990
downloadable option to get a fasta
and flat file view that contains gi

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:27.150
numbers, but they won't be default. and
finally in march of next year, gi's

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:30.780
will not be available at all in sequence
record views.

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:37.590
So what will break right? so what will
these changes - what

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:40.410
consequences will these changes have to a
typical user?

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:45.420
the main issue is to think about any
process that you have that parses GI

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:48.240
numbers from flat files or from fasta.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.040
so if you are using those data records
as a source to get GI numbers out, those

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.730
processes will break, because the GI
numbers will simply not be there anymore.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:12:00.660
So that's the primary thing
to be concerned about at this point.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:06.900
And again you have until march to really
fix those processes and update them, but

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:11.280
that's the most important thing to think
about. Things that will not break. If you

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:16.410
have a database that has gi's in it now
those gi's will continue to be

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:21.510
used as retrieval engines for
identifiers for records ncbi. so

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:26.040
any record that has a GI now will
continue to have that gi, will continue

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:29.460
to be retrievable by that GI
indefinitely.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:35.700
Also the gi's will continue to be in the
xml and ASN.1 presentations of

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:36.750
sequence records.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:39.780
so if you're using XML or the ASN.1,
nothing will change,

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:46.620
just flat files and fasta. However, and
this is a big however, more and more new

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:51.510
sequences will not be assigned gi's and
it's not going to be predictable which

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.600
sequences are going to get gi's and
which ones won't.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:13:01.170
so if you only use gi's, the data that you
will be able to access from NCBI will

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:05.280
become more and more obsolete. Or
less and less newer

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.570
sequences will actually be able to be
represented in a database that only uses

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:14.160
GI's. So again moving to accession.version is going to be a plus for

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.960
keeping up with the most current data. so
our recommendations at this point in

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:19.410
time

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.180
again any process that parses GI'S from
flat files or fasta really must be

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:26.399
fixed as soon as possible,

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:30.990
now preferably. so start working on those,
those are the biggest problem. Any

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:36.420
process or database that depends on gi's,
right, for indexing or for retrieval,

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:40.920
should be updated to depend on accession.version instead.

00:13:41.579 --> 00:13:46.139
So we're recommending that you switch
those primary keys away from gi's to

00:13:46.139 --> 00:13:50.490
accession.versions if you're using
those in some way, and that should be

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:54.810
done as soon as you can possibly do it.
but again the most critical thing is if

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:57.959
you're parsing GI's out of those flat
files or fasta.

00:13:57.959 --> 00:14:03.899
Alright, so there's a number of things
that will be happening in addition and

00:14:03.899 --> 00:14:07.769
these are not all to the point that we
can tell you specifically about them but

00:14:07.769 --> 00:14:12.600
additional webinars, blog post
announcements will cover these topics.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:17.699
But here are some things that we can
generally expect in the future. In

00:14:17.699 --> 00:14:21.899
September or around september we
expect a new parameter to be available

00:14:21.899 --> 00:14:26.819
for the E-utilities, these are the
Entrez API's, that will

00:14:26.819 --> 00:14:31.620
allow them to accept accession.version
as input and provide accession.version

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:38.160
as outputs. so currently the E-utilities
use GI numbers as their primary keys for

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:42.360
retrieval of sequence data. That
obviously is not going to be a great

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:43.139
idea

00:14:43.139 --> 00:14:48.660
moving forward. so we will have an option
to switch the mode in the E-utilities to

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:52.800
use accession.version instead. the
GI version will continue to be the

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:57.089
default and will continue to function
for all sequence records that currently

00:14:57.089 --> 00:15:01.800
have GI'S, but to access all of the data
you would need to use this new parameter

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.480
to switch to accession.version mode,
if you will, the E-utilities to access data.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:14.279
EDirect is a fairly recent package that
allows you to use the E-utilities from

00:15:14.279 --> 00:15:16.199
the unix command line.

00:15:16.199 --> 00:15:20.129
this is described in detail in the E-utilities
documentation, i'm not going to

00:15:20.129 --> 00:15:24.240
go into that right now, but that will
also be updated to handle accession.version

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:27.839
input/output and that should be
also in the September time frame.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:33.620
we understand that in many cases what
these kinds of changes would mean is

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:38.089
that you will need to convert gi's to
accession.version.

00:15:38.089 --> 00:15:42.769
We are planning to offer a new
service, again around september, that

00:15:42.769 --> 00:15:47.149
would be able to do this in large
batches. So, and by large I mean around a

00:15:47.149 --> 00:15:53.360
hundred thousand or more at a time, so if
you do have a large set of gi's and just

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:57.110
want to switch them over to accession.
version we are going to have a service

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:58.850
available for you to do that.

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:04.730
In addition we plan to have some ftp
files that would have the entire set of

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:09.529
gi's that would then associate the
correct accession.version with that GI.

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:16.100
those are fairly large files, they will be
available by ftp. finally in September we

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:20.390
expect that a new version of the blast
binaries will be coming out that will be

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:24.589
using accession.version as opposed
to gi's and we'll have more to say about

00:16:24.589 --> 00:16:28.940
blast as events unfold. but those are
some things that we're expecting to

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.970
happen in the relatively near future
around September.

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:35.870
So how do you keep abreast of this, how
do you keep in the know,

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:39.980
tell your friends and neighbors about
things that are happening related to

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:45.140
these changes? Please follow our blog
that's NCBI insights, that's 

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.930
ncbiinsights.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:50.899
there's links on our home page there's
links in these

00:16:50.899 --> 00:16:55.520
presentation slides, the NCBI News site,
our social media, facebook and

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:00.560
twitter accounts, all of these are again
linked from the NCBI homepage and from the

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:05.030
NCBI news page also. We will be
announcing all of these changes on those

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:06.350
platforms.

00:17:06.350 --> 00:17:10.939
so that's a great way of keeping track
and keeping updated on what's happening.

00:17:10.939 --> 00:17:14.990
we will be giving additional webinars so
watch out for those. those will be on the

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:16.250
Learn page.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:20.630
the URL is right there and if you are
interested in the API is or the

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:25.939
E-utilities, it's a great idea to sign up
for utilities announce, and the link is

00:17:25.939 --> 00:17:29.240
there to where you can sign up to that
mailing list. it's just a simple email

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:33.500
list where we send out all the detailed
announcements about upcoming changes to

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:34.880
the E-utilities.

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:38.750
And finally if you have questions about
this that we haven't covered today or if

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:41.570
you have questions down the road please
feel free to write to info@ncbi

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:44.809
and we'll certainly be happy to
address those questions.

00:17:45.889 --> 00:17:49.669
All right so I'm going to take a break
at this point see if there are questions

00:17:49.669 --> 00:17:52.549
that we have to address.

00:17:52.549 --> 00:17:57.049
So there's several questions
basically about the E-utilities.

00:17:57.049 --> 00:18:00.380
There's a lot of people concerned
about you know how are they going to

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:04.309
work with the new accession.version
formats, so can you address that a little

00:18:04.309 --> 00:18:04.970
bit?

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:09.080
What we expect right now and again this
is what I was talking about in terms of

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:15.049
a new parameter that would be coming out
for the E-utilities, is that the parameter

00:18:15.049 --> 00:18:20.899
at this point we believe will be called
idtype. So idtype parameter when it's

00:18:20.899 --> 00:18:23.299
set to a value of acc,

00:18:23.299 --> 00:18:30.740
so you'd add an idtype equals
acc parameter to any E-utility URL, and that

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:36.320
will convert the E-utility into a mode
where it expects to receive accession.

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:40.309
versions and where it will output
accession.version. So a couple of

00:18:40.309 --> 00:18:46.100
examples. In esearch for example, esearch typically takes an Entrez query

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:50.720
into like nuccore protein, one of those
databases, and returns to you a list of

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.669
gi's right?

00:18:52.669 --> 00:18:57.529
We don't want that, we want accession.version, so if you were to add idtype

00:18:57.529 --> 00:19:01.639
equals acc, what will happen is you do
the same search using esearch, the

00:19:01.639 --> 00:19:06.019
same term, but then what you'll get back
in the XML will be accession.

00:19:06.019 --> 00:19:08.509
versions rather than the gi numbers.

00:19:08.509 --> 00:19:14.750
Similarly in efetch or esummary you
would be able to provide accession.

00:19:14.750 --> 00:19:20.240
versions to the ID parameter. Now in
efetch, just as a trick, you can do that

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:20.960
now.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:26.539
So efetch does take accession.
versions in the ID parameter now, and it

00:19:26.539 --> 00:19:28.759
has for actually a few years.

00:19:28.759 --> 00:19:33.110
so you can get that support currently.
Esummary does not.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:38.600
so esummary only takes gi's currently
so esummary would be changed so that

00:19:38.600 --> 00:19:42.169
if you use idtype equals acc, esummary
would be able to take accession.versions

00:19:42.169 --> 00:19:47.269
and give you back the doc sums in
xml for those records. And then finally

00:19:47.269 --> 00:19:52.399
for eink we would expect that elink
would be able to take the accession.

00:19:52.399 --> 00:19:54.919
versions and then if you're linking

00:19:54.919 --> 00:19:59.359
into a database, for example linking from
nucleotide protein or from genome to

00:19:59.359 --> 00:20:04.820
protein or from PubMed to nuccore, the output
of elink would be in accession.version

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.529
rather than GI.

00:20:06.529 --> 00:20:10.940
So those are our expectations that's
being worked on right now and we expect

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:14.899
that to be available publicly sometime
in the september time frame.

00:20:14.899 --> 00:20:19.039
There are a few other questions, Eric,
that are sort of asking about whether

00:20:19.039 --> 00:20:22.070
they're going to have fasta files
available for testing? With the deflines

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:26.809
that are without the GI numbers in
them. And also sort of about like when

00:20:26.809 --> 00:20:32.840
will people be expecting fasta files on
the ftp site to change to the new format?

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:39.109
Good questions. So and in terms of the
availability of test files, that is on

00:20:39.109 --> 00:20:44.629
our agenda to provide. We expect to be
able to provide some sample

00:20:44.629 --> 00:20:48.649
genbank flat files for nucleotide and
protein as well as fasta files in the

00:20:48.649 --> 00:20:53.570
near future so keep abreast of the
announcements and we'll announce where

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:54.859
those are coming.

00:20:54.859 --> 00:20:59.869
I would point you again, if you would
like, just take a look and that news

00:20:59.869 --> 00:21:06.049
article that i showed earlier. It does
have examples of the places in the flat

00:21:06.049 --> 00:21:10.190
files and fasta where the gi's are
currently and where they will not be,

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:14.749
alright, in the new in the new data
formats. So even though it's not a full

00:21:14.749 --> 00:21:18.590
record it would give you a bit of an
idea about what to expect. But we will be

00:21:18.590 --> 00:21:24.230
putting some sample data out so that you
can test with that. In terms of other

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:28.369
presentation formats that are not
directly from Entrez, for example like

00:21:28.369 --> 00:21:34.070
ftp files, the genbank releases, RefSeq
releases, the returns that you would get

00:21:34.070 --> 00:21:38.299
from the E-utilities, these kinds of
questions. We expect that all of these

00:21:38.299 --> 00:21:39.679
data formats

00:21:39.679 --> 00:21:44.779
if they're flat files or fasta, will
again not have the gi numbers after

00:21:44.779 --> 00:21:46.970
march of 2017.

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:51.590
So that's the drop-dead date. After that
point you will not see GI numbers in any

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:57.889
of the releases of RefSeq, genbank or any of
the other sequence ftp files that we

00:21:57.889 --> 00:22:02.299
have nor any of the returns from the
E-utilities or Entrez.

00:22:02.299 --> 00:22:08.330
Beginning in September we will begin to
remove the gi's from default presentations.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:13.159
The first priority with that is going to
be on the web and Entrez presentations

00:22:13.159 --> 00:22:16.669
so that's the first thing likely to happen.

00:22:16.669 --> 00:22:21.110
I'm not going to be able to give you a
date right now about when those changes

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:24.289
will happen in genbank and RefSeq
releases, except that it will happen

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:29.600
by March of 2017. I think those are the
general topics. There's an interesting

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.570
one that I don't know the answer to for
sure.

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:36.890
We've simplified the defline in
another way besides removing the GI

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:40.700
number, this abbreviation for the
database in the fasta defline has gone

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:44.809
away. Now I assume that's largely just to
make things simpler although one person

00:22:44.809 --> 00:22:48.590
in the questions pod is concerned about
that because they find it makes it

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:51.919
easier to understand the feature table
format.

00:22:51.919 --> 00:22:56.870
Yes thanks for the question. it is true
that the current proposal is to remove

00:22:56.870 --> 00:23:01.549
the gb or the ref moniker there that
would let you know what the source

00:23:01.549 --> 00:23:06.889
database was. At this point one of the
driving forces for the simplification is

00:23:06.889 --> 00:23:12.169
any other third party software
applications and other processes accept

00:23:12.169 --> 00:23:15.230
a very simple def line with one
identifier more easily.

00:23:15.799 --> 00:23:18.950
that's one of the things that we're
trying to respond to. But I'll take that

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:23.960
under advisement. If there's interest in
having some indication about the

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:27.260
source database, that has come up in some
discussions.

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:32.600
One thing I would say is that in many
cases you can determine from the

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:36.830
accession number format what the source
database is. So the presence of an

00:23:36.830 --> 00:23:41.870
underscore in the accession is a dead
ringer that's a RefSeq and that's pretty

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:47.029
much universal rule. Without underscores it's
almost certainly going to be an ISNDC

00:23:47.029 --> 00:23:48.110
sequence,

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:54.380
genbank, embl, ddbj. You can also determine
tsa and wgs sequences by the fact that there

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:58.700
are 4 alpha characters in the beginning
of the accession. So there are some rules

00:23:58.700 --> 00:24:03.139
that you can use. it may be that we want
to publicize those and tabulate those

00:24:03.139 --> 00:24:04.460
little bit more clearly

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:08.330
so that people can see those and use
them to determine sources.

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:12.649
All right, well thank you everyone for your
attention and for attending today and

00:24:12.649 --> 00:24:15.529
again please write to us if you have
additional questions in the meantime.

00:24:15.529 --> 00:24:17.029
Thank you very much.

